@chapter 15 | Metals, Ores and Alloys

@intro
This chapter covers the **properties of metals** (hardest, heaviest, liquid at room temperature, best conductor, metals kept under kerosene), **ores and minerals** (bauxite, haematite, calamine, cinnabar, gypsum, limestone, plaster of Paris, mica), **corrosion** (rusting, galvanising, tarnishing of silver) and **alloys** (brass, bronze, solder, amalgam, German silver, stainless steel). It contains **{pyqs} PYQs and {ones} one-liners**.
@end

@section 15.1 | Properties of Metals

@q 19:1

@src 19:2
@pyq U.P. Lower Sub. (Pre) 2004
The heaviest natural element is ?
(a) Uranium
(b) Mercury
(c) Gold
(d) Calcium
@ans (a)
@end

Among metals, **tungsten** and **chromium** are among the hardest (diamond, a non-metal, is the hardest natural substance). **Uranium** (atomic mass 238) is the heaviest naturally abundant element; the **densest** metal is **osmium** (22.6 g/cm^{3}), followed by iridium. **Tungsten** has the highest melting point of all metals (3,422°C).

@pyq U.P. P.C.S. (Pre) 2016
The costliest metal of the world discovered recently is :
(a) Endohedral Fullerene
(b) Californium 252
(c) Tritium
(d) Rhodium
@ans (*)
@note **Californium-252** (made in reactors, used as a neutron source) is often quoted as the costliest element, and **rhodium** is the costliest precious metal traded. *Endohedral fullerenes* (carbon cages holding atoms) are not metals but are extremely costly; none was "discovered recently", so the source marks the question (*).
@end
@src 19:3

@q 19:4-5

@src 19:6
@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 2007
Sodium Vapor Lamp is usually used as street light, because –
(a) These are cheap.
(b) Light from this is monochromatic and will not split through water droplets.
(c) It is pleasing to the eyes.
(d) It is brightly illuminating.
@ans (b)
@end

**Sodium** and **potassium** react violently with water and air, so they are stored under **kerosene**; **white phosphorus** is stored under water. Sodium lamps give **monochromatic yellow** light (589 nm) that is not dispersed by fog droplets, so it is good for street lighting.

@q 19:12-13

@src1 19:1

@one
Which of the following metals is in liquid state at normal temperature?
@ans Mercury
@end

**Mercury** is the only metal **liquid at room temperature** (gallium and caesium melt just above it). It is used in thermometers because it expands evenly, does not stick to glass, is opaque and shiny, and works from -39°C to 357°C. It is a good conductor of heat among liquids.

